A||N||9||Monkeyman||I HATE MY JOB !!!!!!!!!||||8||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||08-04-2000||02:17 PM||||It's too bad arson is illegal!  I'm at the point of burning down the place.  I was just offered a ticket to the Brickyard 400 this morning ($125--box seat).  Unfortunately, I had to turn it down.<P>For those of you that don't know my job...I'm the asst mgr at a convenience store.  We only have 2 of us currently assigned to the store.  I work 7 days/nights a week so there's no way I could take the night off or call in sick.  No one would be there to fill in.  I've wanted to attend the Brickyard 400 for 7 years and, now that I get the chance, I can't take it!  The other employee (I hate to say "worker") is one of the laziest B****s I have ever met!  It takes threats just to get her to show up when she's scheduled to work.  There's no way she'd ever show up in a situation like this.  I try to see the good in everyone, but I'm having trouble finding any in her.  <P>One of the reasons I quit driving truck was to spend more time at home.  Yeah.  Right.  I'm gone from home almost as many hours now as I was then.  For about 1/2 the money.  I know...If I hate the job so much, why don't I find another.  There are 2 reasons.  1st, I'm probably going to be the manager at the other Village Pantry in town once the new one opens.  (This has been my goal all along.)  And, 2nd, I've never been good at staying at one job very long.  I've only been fired once, but usually when I get good and pi**ed, I'll just quit and find another.  There have been years when I've had as many as 10 jobs in a single year.  I keep hoping I'll grow up and learn to put up with some of the horse crap in the world and stick with a job, so...here I am.  The pay sucks (can we say minimum wage?), the benefits aren't all that great,  the hours are...mmm...let's just say that I haven't had a chance to sleep in my own bed in a while.  I just pile up on the couch for a couple of hours.  This comes real close to being the final straw.  I know that it's not VPs fault that I was offered a ticket.  It's just one more thing on the list.  There aren't many jobs locally, other than factory jobs (I'm just not cut out for that).  Like a fool, I keep coming back for more.  I do all the physical work, too.  <P>Let me give you another example of the stupidity of the place.  We have a fill in manager from another store this week.  Since we will be closing the store on the 21st, we were instructed to order only what was absolutely necessary.  I ordered a total of 37 cartons of cigarettes.  This moron decided to redo the order.  Instead of ordering what I had wrote down, he read the wrong line in the order book and ordered what we had on hand.  <B>224 cartons</B>!!!!!!  We have some cigarettes that we've had for 5 months.  And he ordered another 16 cartons!<P>Sorry for being so long winded, but I just had to vent.<P>Thanx for "listening".<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||8
Z||000001||BN Boomer||08-04-2000||03:50 PM||||I don't suppose that you want to hear that I like my job as a railroad switchman and make about $50k per year for working less than 35 hours per week, huh?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ||||reg||12
Z||000002||frontal lobe||08-04-2000||06:43 PM||||Monkeyman, the key issue here is when is the new store opening so that you will be moved to manage it.  At least if you have a reasonable idea of when that is, and whether you will likely be the manager, at least there is "light at the end of the tunnel".  Otherwise, you are in a dead end job that no matter WHAT job you switched to, there is no way it could be worse.<P>Is there a Target store in the area?  My brother worked his way up through Target and said they were a good company to work for.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||08-04-2000||06:49 PM||||I know where you're coming from dude. I've had jobs I hated too. When you get good and ready, you'll make a change. I'm like you, I've had a hundered jobs in my life, some good, some sucked worse than others. I've always heard, do what you love, and the money will follow. The problem is, that takes more confidence in the way the universe works than most people can come up with. I am living proof of that theory though. I gave up a growing (read: dying LOL) contracting business to go into business as a training consultant. I did this type of work while in the Navy, and loved it, but found no real market on the outside. I now work whenever I want to, and make more money than I ever made before. On my current project, I write learning guides for AEP and Ohio State University. It takes me anywhere from a day to three days to write one guide. I email the completed guide to my editor, and fax him an invoice. This means I can work from ANYWHERE there's a phone line. And each invoice I send is for a minumum of a thousand dollars per guide. The last guide and invoice I sent from my girlfriends' house. The one before that I sent from my dad's house in WV. In september, I'll be sending a guide and invoice from Pensacola Florida. Just open yourself to the possibilities; dreams DO come true dude. <P>Sorry about the length, didn't mean to get preachy!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>-Taiji<p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 08-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||SCCA FIERO||08-04-2000||06:50 PM||||I can make you feel better Monkey. I work in the blazing hot sun... it was 95 today and 103 a couple days ago. I work outside, carrying heavy things all day, and am beat when I get home. I hate my job, but I am trapped in it. I can't afford to do anything else because I would never make the money I am right now. If I did I would probably have to sell everything I own. I think I am getting skin cancer... no joke! Not to mention it gets to about 0 to 15 degrees on a hot day here in the winter. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fret Naught||08-04-2000||07:26 PM||||Time to pack it in Monkeyman, go West that is Northwest lots of jobs out here everywhere I go I see we are hiring signs.<BR>Wal-Mart Home Base Home Depot Costco,<BR>Don't laugh they may seem like nowwhere places, but I have a friend that works at Home Depot, and the pay and benifits are pretty darn good, even has a pretty decent retirement plan.||||reg||14
Z||000006||gixxer||08-04-2000||08:00 PM||||I agree with Fret Naught. Up here in the Pacific Northwest (Seattle area) there are lots of jobs that are going unfilled. You have your pick. <BR>Everybody I know seems to have a job.<BR>Cost of living is high 'tho.<BR>  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||stimpy||08-04-2000||08:22 PM||||I'm tellin ya, I think you need to come check out Oklahoma City. One of the cheapest cost of living in the US<BR>combined with a very low unemployment rate makes it very attractive. There are several call center locations including Southwest Airlines, Hertz, DirectTV, AOL... If you speak english, can pass a drug test and have no criminal background, you are only unemployed cuz you want to be!<P>BTW, are you going to be having a good sale on smokes soon?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Put me down for a couple of cartons of Marlboro Lights!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://gifs123.tripod.com/page15/renstimpy2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||hugh||08-04-2000||09:43 PM||||Dave,The cream rises to the top.If you do your best you'll be successful at whatever you do.I gave up a great job to go into business with my 2 brothers in 1973.I'm 60 now and working harder than ever,but I wouldn't change anything that has happened.What I am trying to say is,you sound like the type of person that would be successful in your own business.Look around and don't be afraid to take a chance.It's better than saying"I wish I had"||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||08-05-2000||02:23 AM||||Wow!  Thanx, guys.  If everything goes according to plan (when does it ever), I will be the new manager of the soon to be old Village Pantry (as soon as the new one opens--mid October?).  <B>If</B> that happens, everything will be worth it.  If not, I'll cry a little bit, cuss out my boss and move on.  I have alot of passions, but none that I am any good at.  I think that management will, if nothing else, make me feel better about myself.  I've always thought that the world should be fair.  Of course, it isn't.  This might be my chance to make a tiny part of it fair.  I told my boss today that I don't care if my employees like me as long as they can honestly say that I am fair and honest.  I'll keep everyone informed.||||reg||1
